GLFW is written in C language, it doesn't know about C++ objects
   - glfwSetWindowUserPointer(glfwWindow, this);
   - makes the current object known by the c++ object you use
   - TerminalWindow* tw = static_cast<TerminalWindow*>(glfwGetWindowUserPointer(window));
   - get the object now know by glfw

nullptr def:
   In c++ a null pointer is used for good practice when defining
   a pointer that is not initialized a value.
